<aside startOpen="true"><title>Stability theorem</title>

<p>Let <m>\frac{ dx }{ dt } = f(x)</m> be an autonomous differential equation.  Suppose <m>x(t)=x^*</m> is an equilibrium, i.e.,
<m>f(x^*)=0</m>.  Then</p>
<ul>
<li>if <m>f'(x^*)\lt 0</m>, the equilibrium <m>x(t)=x^*</m> is stable, and</li>
<li>if <m>f'(x^*)\gt 0</m>, the equilibrium <m>x(t)=x^*</m> is unstable.</li>
</ul>
</aside>
  
<problem><title>Video review questions</title>
<exercise includeAutoNumber="true"><title />
<p>For an autonomous differential equation, 
  <me>\frac{dx}{dt} = f(x)</me>
the graph of <m>f</m> as a function of the state variable <m>x</m> is graphed below. Drag the points shown in the lower right corner to the equilibrium locations.</p>

  <graph xmin="-6" xmax="6" grid="true">
    <xLabel><m>x</m></xLabel>
    <yLabel><m>\frac{dx}{dt}</m></yLabel>
    <function through="(-4,-4) (-3,0) (-1,4) (1,-4) (3,0) (4,4)" applyStyleToLabel="true">
      <label><m>f(x)</m></label>
    </function>
    <point styleNumber="3" name="dragPoint1">(3,-4)
      <constrainToGrid />
    </point>
    <point styleNumber="3" name="dragPoint2">(4,-4)
      <constrainToGrid />
    </point>
    <point styleNumber="3" name="dragPoint3">(5,-4)
      <constrainToGrid />
    </point>
  </graph>

  <answer name="ans1a" matchPartial="true" unorderedCompare="true">
    <award>
      <when>($dragPoint1.coords, $dragPoint2.coords, $dragPoint3.coords) = ((-3,0),(0,0),(3,0))</when>
    </award>
  </answer>
  <hint><p>The equilibrium points are locations where <m>\frac{dx}{dt} = 0</m>.</p></hint>
</exercise>

<exercise includeAutoNumber="true"><title />  
<p>Which of these points that you located above corresponds to a stable equilibrium?</p>
  <p><m>x_e = </m>
  <answer name="ans1b">
    <choiceInput name="ci">
    <choice><m>$dragPoint1.coords</m></choice>
    <choice><m>$dragPoint2.coords</m></choice>
    <choice><m>$dragPoint3.coords</m></choice>
    </choiceInput>
    <award>
      <when>$ci = (0,0)</when></award>
  </answer>
  </p>

  <hint><p>Remember, when an equilibrium is stable, that means that the trajectories for solutions with initial conditions near the equilbrium point move <em>toward</em> the equilibrium. </p>
    <p>Here, you have to use your imagination to visualize the behavior of solutions <m>x(t)</m> based on the values of it's derivative <m>\frac{dx}{dt}</m> shown in the graph. For a reminder of how to do this, see <ref to="doenet:7Y8muMcnPJ2z7QYSakZySD">Solving single autonomous differential equations using graphical methods </ref></p></hint>



</exercise>
</problem>

</section>


